GEOMETRY  cube side 0.0065782287 m; face 0.43273093 cm^2; sphere projected area pi R^2 0.52316908 cm^2

(1) MUONS  PDG 2020 Cosmic Rays review (explore/data/pdg2020_cosmic_rays.txt lines 308-311):
    vertical integral intensity E>1 GeV/c at sea level ~ 70 m^-2 s^-1 sr^-1 = 0.0070 cm^-2 s^-1 sr^-1; recent measurements favour 10-15% lower; angular law cos^2(theta); "~1 cm^-2 min^-1 for horizontal detectors"
    PDG 70             (pi/2) I A face: 0.00475813 s^-1 = 0.285488 /min = 411.102 /day
    PDG 70 minus 10%   (pi/2) I A face: 0.00428231 s^-1 = 0.256939 /min = 369.992 /day
    PDG 70 minus 15%   (pi/2) I A face: 0.00404441 s^-1 = 0.242664 /min = 349.437 /day
    PDG "1 cm^-2 min^-1" (all energies, horizontal) x face: 0.00721218 s^-1 = 0.432731 /min = 623.133 /day
    ratio of that to the E>1 GeV cos^2 integral (PDG 70): 1.51576  -> the rate depends on the energy threshold at the factor 1.5 level, and on a flux normalization at 10-15%
    the cos^2 integral itself: 2 pi I int cos^3 sin d theta = (pi/2) I exactly; a different exponent n gives 2 pi I/(n+2): n=2 -> pi/2 = 1.5708, n=2.0 vs 1.8 changes it by 0.05263
    range carried for the gram: 0.2427 - 0.4327 per minute (a factor 1.78 )

(2) u, d MASSES  PDG 2024 (pdg2024_quarks.txt): mu = 2.16 +- 0.07 MeV, md = 4.70 +- 0.07 MeV (90% CL), mu/md = 0.462 +- 0.020, (mu+md)/2 = 3.49 +- 0.07 MeV; all MSbar at 2 GeV
    valence 18u+18d at PDG central: 123.48 MeV = 1.10468 % of the atom rest energy
    PDG linear extremes (+-0.07 each, fully correlated): 120.96 - 126.0 MeV = 1.08213 - 1.12722 %
    PDG (mu+md)/2 = 3.49 +- 0.07 -> 36 x that = 125.64 MeV; consistent with 18(mu+md)
    lattice cross-check: FLAG m_ud = 3.364(41) MeV (Nf=2+1, via arXiv:2003.11703 as quoted in a search result, secondary) with PDG mu/md=0.462 ->
      mu = 2.12608  md = 4.60192 MeV; sum 18(mu+md) = 121.104 MeV = 1.08342 % ; difference from PDG central -0.01924
    scale dependence (ILLUSTRATIVE, one-loop, inputs not from a saved file): m(mu) ~ alpha_s(mu)^(4/9) for nf=3; with alpha_s(1,2,3 GeV) = 0.47, 0.30, 0.25 (rough):
      mu_scale 1 GeV: factor 1.2208 -> 18(mu+md) = 150.748 MeV = 1.3486 %
      mu_scale 2 GeV: factor 1.0 -> 18(mu+md) = 123.48 MeV = 1.1047 %
      mu_scale 3 GeV: factor 0.92216 -> 18(mu+md) = 113.869 MeV = 1.0187 %
    the quark mass sum is a convention-dependent number; the reading "1.1% of the atom is bare quark mass" is good to about 2-3% of itself (statistical) and moves about 20% with the scale convention; the other ~99% is the proton/neutron mass that is not the bare quark masses

